531
00:43:43,620 --> 00:44:31,590
Speaker 0: And that's a whole domain that's probably outside of bounds for this show but that is the effective use of widgets because that is the case where you have people whose job it is to do something like monitor a nuclear power plant, monitor an acid bath in a factory, monitor patients in a hospital and I would always characterize it as a situation where alerts alone aren't good enough because the people in the console room are humans who are intelligent who will need to very quickly look at the state of the world and decide if there's a problem or not likely by cross-referencing some information on these widgets like the current temperature of the cold room and other information they know like someone just called me from the cold room saying they think there's a problem with the compressor.

532
00:44:33,410 --> 00:44:34,669
Speaker 1: Yeah, so it's like the thing is.

533
00:44:35,013 --> 00:44:44,001
Speaker 1: some of those, you know, they're usually widgets that are like part of a dashboard like someone will create like a whole screen worth of widgets that are always open, always on.

534
00:44:44,001 --> 00:44:45,678
Speaker 0: 99% of what Scott's describing are bullshit.

535
00:44:45,839 --> 00:44:49,476
Speaker 0: Most widgets in a console room are designed by people who have no idea what they're doing.

536
00:44:49,860 --> 00:44:50,507
Speaker 0: They're useless.

537
00:44:50,568 --> 00:44:52,069
Speaker 0: You could solve that in another way.

538
00:44:53,474 --> 00:44:56,510
Speaker 0: A screen that just shows everything's green is almost never useful.

539
00:44:57,570 --> 00:45:05,211
Speaker 1: Right, it's like, you know, those are often more for like showing off and making it look like everything's cool.

540
00:45:05,312 --> 00:45:06,019
Speaker 0: Oh, what was the word?

541
00:45:06,039 --> 00:45:08,404
Speaker 0: We had a word for that at Pipeline.

542
00:45:08,464 --> 00:45:12,102
Speaker 0: Back when I worked on the cell side, we literally, we had a word.

543
00:45:12,122 --> 00:45:13,417
Speaker 0: I don't remember what it was.

544
00:45:13,437 --> 00:45:14,287
Speaker 0: It'll come to me.

545
00:45:14,810 --> 00:45:24,930
Speaker 0: For the things we put up on the big monitors that were above us that we never looked at but really impressed people who came into our room to talk to us like executives.

546
00:45:25,310 --> 00:45:25,655
Speaker 1: Right.

547
00:45:25,675 --> 00:45:36,569
Speaker 1: I mean, if you think about, you know, something like Evangelion, it's like, you know, these, you get a room where these people are like sitting at computers and like you can see all of them are focused on the computer in front of them.

548
00:45:37,293 --> 00:45:46,629
Speaker 1: But all around the room are all these monitors going like with awesome graphics and typography and famous, you know, Evangelion computer graphic style on them.

549
00:45:48,542 --> 00:45:51,010
Speaker 1: Yeah, I feel like Star Trek had some of the same shit, right?

550
00:45:51,130 --> 00:45:57,130
Speaker 1: Everyone on the bridge is focused on their shit, but around the room, there's like all these other cool looking Star Trek displays.

551
00:45:57,693 --> 00:45:58,585
Speaker 1: They're not looking at them.

552
00:45:58,646 --> 00:46:00,428
Speaker 1: They're just there to look cool.

553
00:46:01,730 --> 00:46:13,717
Speaker 1: But sometimes, so dashboards I think when they're well designed, people are looking at them, you know, and it's like it's someone's job to is like to actually look to watch the status and react to it in real time.

554
00:46:14,362 --> 00:46:17,474
Speaker 1: Then the dashboard with all the widgets on it is useful.

555
00:46:17,919 --> 00:46:25,758
Speaker 1: But a lot of times people just like have them open as sort of like background noise almost just for style or something.

556
00:46:25,859 --> 00:46:32,139
Speaker 0: And that's where like that was literally my job like for most of my career over a couple decades was that exact thing.

557
00:46:32,726 --> 00:46:48,569
Speaker 0: And I guess really simply what I can say is dashboards showing information should only show information that is necessary for a human to synthesize with other information that is not in that dashboard to make rapid critical decisions.

558
00:46:49,750 --> 00:46:57,494
Speaker 1: Yeah, there was a customer at a place I worked and they had a software open on that.

559
00:46:57,515 --> 00:47:05,054
Speaker 1: basically it was like it was a big company and all these people would come into work and they would all open up this dashboard on their computer and they set it to the highest refresh rate.

560
00:47:06,085 --> 00:47:07,358
Speaker 1: All of them at the same time.

561
00:47:07,964 --> 00:47:14,389
Speaker 1: And I don't it didn't really seem like they all really needed it to be at that high refresh rate or they all needed it to be that open all the time.

562
00:47:14,913 --> 00:47:20,130
Speaker 1: But it basically just caused the same queries to go to the database over and over and over and over and over and over constantly.

563
00:47:22,057 --> 00:47:24,209
Speaker 1: But I guess we had to account for that.

564
00:47:24,710 --> 00:47:35,418
Speaker 0: The use case of these things it's that human synthesis that's important because if you could detect if something went wrong then you don't need the thing saying "green good".

565
00:47:35,499 --> 00:47:40,070
Speaker 0: instead you need an alert that tells everyone "Hey, alert!

566
00:47:40,530 --> 00:47:41,176
Speaker 0: Something bad

567
00:47:41,217 --> 00:47:41,641
Speaker 0: happened.".

568
00:47:42,086 --> 00:47:45,829
Speaker 0: And if you need humans to make decisions then you need to show them the information they need to make decisions.

569
00:47:46,455 --> 00:47:54,295
Speaker 0: So like that's the line will a human use this to synthesize with other information in their human brain to make a human decision.

570
00:47:54,617 --> 00:48:00,788
Speaker 0: If you could put all the information in the display and you don't need the human to synthesize then you don't need the dashboard in the first place.

571
00:48:01,773 --> 00:48:09,104
Speaker 1: So I think that overall this kind of widget it's good that it exists because there are occasions where it is very important.

572
00:48:09,124 --> 00:48:10,215
Speaker 1: you know it is actually useful.

573
00:48:10,255 --> 00:48:20,050
Speaker 1: it is saving you time the frequently accessed important actionable information is ready at hand just a click away or always always visible.

574
00:48:20,957 --> 00:48:45,945
Speaker 1: but I think a lot of people turn on a lot of widgets that they're not actually looking at that don't have actionable frequently referenced information because for some other reason and I would encourage you to evaluate the usage of your precious screen real estate because that honestly a screen with like empty space you know just like breathing is you know probably feels better than just cluttering it up with a bunch of useless nonsense.

575
00:48:46,046 --> 00:48:47,885
Speaker 0: absolutely I think there's two or three.

576
00:48:47,905 --> 00:48:51,978
Speaker 0: I think one reason people do this is they think it looks cool either consciously or subconsciously.

577
00:48:53,275 --> 00:49:13,729
Speaker 0: I think another reason is people feel like they're unproductive and they have been tricked into thinking that this is a way to make themselves more productive or focused or something and like they just buy into like oh well if I put this widget here then it'll make me do my Duolingo more often like just click on Duolingo once a day and also the owl yells at you if you don't do the Duolingo you don't need a widget.

578
00:49:15,617 --> 00:49:22,534
Speaker 0: and I think a third reason people do this is apps push them into doing it and they assume that's just how you use computers.

579
00:49:23,282 --> 00:49:27,750
Speaker 1: well I mean I think the thing is if you are someone who makes an app you would love for someone.

580
00:49:28,214 --> 00:49:40,170
Speaker 1: if even if your widget largely doesn't make any sense you would love for someone to put your widget on their homepage to you know because now you get how much more space on their homepage or their home screen on their phone.

581
00:49:40,271 --> 00:49:42,150
Speaker 1: is you and not someone else right?

582
00:49:42,493 --> 00:49:46,409
Speaker 1: it's like all you care about is aha I have an icon that's 4x the other icons.

583
00:49:46,955 --> 00:49:48,450
Speaker 1: I'm crushing my competition.

584
